WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.520
Your brain is about 2 % of your weight.

2
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:09.300
It uses about 20 % of the energy you spend at rest.

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.200
Much of that goes to axons and synapses.

4
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:16.600
A motor axon can run a metre, and myelin wraps most of it.

5
00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:19.500
Myelin limits access to the fluid outside.

6
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.900
So how is the far end fed?

7
00:00:22.833 --> 00:00:23.893
Let's Start with the cost.

8
00:00:24.473 --> 00:00:28.193
Every action potential lets sodium in and potassium out.

9
00:00:28.713 --> 00:00:31.053
The sodium -potassium pump puts them back.

10
00:00:31.613 --> 00:00:36.553
It spends one ATP to move three sodium out and two potassium in.

11
00:00:37.153 --> 00:00:42.053
Energy budgets for rodent grey matter put most signalling energy into this pumping.

12
00:00:42.673 --> 00:00:43.613
The pumps never rest.

13
00:00:43.613 --> 00:00:47.933
An axon cannot send ATP a metre, so it must make it locally.

14
00:00:48.967 --> 00:00:52.247
Glial cells make myelin in segments along the axon.

15
00:00:52.587 --> 00:00:54.227
That makes them candidate suppliers.

16
00:00:54.927 --> 00:00:58.427
Glucose becomes pyruvate, and pyruvate can become lactate.

17
00:00:58.827 --> 00:01:00.707
That reaction runs in both directions.

18
00:01:01.487 --> 00:01:05.207
Lactate crosses membranes on transporters called MCTs.

19
00:01:05.587 --> 00:01:09.187
A cell that takes up lactate can turn it back into pyruvate and burn it.

20
00:01:09.547 --> 00:01:11.287
So lactate is a portable fuel.

21
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.000
Astrocytes are the other candidate.

22
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:18.660
In cultured mouse astrocytes, glutamate made the cells take up more of a glucose

23
00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.600
analogue and release lactate.

24
00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:22.540
From this came the shuttle idea.

25
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.900
An active synapse makes its astrocyte release lactate, and the neuron burns it.

26
00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:33.620
Some work in mouse cortex reported a lactate gradient from astrocytes to neurons.

27
00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:39.780
But imaging of single neurons saw them raise their own glycolysis, with no sign of

28
00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:44.340
lactate import. So treat the shuttle as a hypothesis with partial support.

29
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.740
The case is firmer for oligodendrocytes.

30
00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.840
Delete COX -10 in these cells, and they can no longer respire.

31
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.060
They live on glycolysis alone.

32
00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:59.300
Even so, in the adult brain, myelin and axons were largely preserved.

33
00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:03.240
That fits glia feeding axons, but does not show it.

34
00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:05.620
The stronger evidence came from the transporter.

35
00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:09.660
Lower MCT1, and motor neurons died.

36
00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:12.840
In mice, axons also swelled.

37
00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.320
In culture, lactate protected stressed neurons.

38
00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:18.390
Here is the picture.

39
00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:25.010
Oligodendrocytes take up glucose, run glycolysis, and export lactate or pyruvate

40
00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:30.330
through MCT1. The axon takes it in and burns it in its mitochondria.

41
00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:34.690
In mice, myelinating glia appear to support axon metabolism.

42
00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:38.410
The route and the size of the contribution are still unsettled.

43
00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:40.670
The astrocyte shuttle is still debated.
